        "Step 1, freeze the discovery setting and state the primary hypothesis: within a fixed test family of earth-abundant oxides, oxynitrides, and oxyfluorides with known structure prototypes, structured joint conditioning on stability and perceptual-gamut-boundary novelty yields more confirmed new usable pigments per fixed synthesis budget than unstructured or single-constraint sampling. Freeze the training data, generative model, property predictors, candidate count, synthesis platform, characterization panel, and acceptance criteria before generating candidates.",
        "Step 2, Phase 1 one-factor test: vary only the sampling-constraint structure while holding all other factors fixed. Create five arms with equal candidate numbers, for example 120 candidates per arm: A random prior sampling, B stability-only posterior, C perceptual-novelty-only posterior, D unstructured mixture of candidates from B and C pooled before final filtering, and E structured joint posterior. All arms use the same latent model, same number of synthesis attempts, same target particle-size range, same binders or application media, and same blinded evaluation.",
        "Step 3, controls for attribution: compare E with D to test whether structured joint conditioning, not mere presence of both constraints or post-hoc filtering, improves yield; compare E with B and C to test the contribution of each constraint; compare E with A to test unstructured exploration. The same latent model and training data are used across arms, holding model capacity and historical-data bias constant. Randomize synthesis order and blind analysts to arm identity so that differences are not caused by operator preference or unequal effort.",
